<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Key Results</strong>: Th=
is approach achieved <strong>&lt;16ms render times</strong> even with 100+ =
simultaneous price updates, <strong>reduced memory usage by 60%</strong>, a=
nd maintained <strong>smooth 60fps performance</strong> during peak trading=
 hours.</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>For KYC applications</st=
rong>, these patterns would be particularly valuable for <strong>real-time =
document verification status updates</strong>, <strong>dynamic form validat=
ion</strong>, and <strong>compliance dashboard refreshes</strong> where use=
rs expect immediate feedback without UI lag.</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Interviewer</strong>: Ex=
cellent deep dive into performance optimization! Your concurrent features u=
sage and performance monitoring approach are exactly what we need for our c=
ompliance dashboards. Now let's talk about React architecture patterns. In =
KYC flows, we often deal with complex, multi-step processes that can branch=
 based on user data, document types, and regulatory requirements. How do yo=
u approach building maintainable, scalable component architectures for such=
 complex workflows? What patterns do you use for prop drilling, state lifti=
ng, and component composition?</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Candidate</strong>: <str=
ong>Complex workflow architecture</strong> is one of the most challenging a=
spects of building scalable React applications, especially in compliance-he=
avy domains like KYC. I've developed a <strong>layered architectural approa=
ch</strong> that emphasizes <strong>separation of concerns</strong>, <stron=
g>predictable data flow</strong>, and <strong>maximum reusability</strong>.=
</p>
